fact WorkItem(id: "WI-876", description: some(value: "THE ORDERED/PARTIALORD COMPARISON SURFACE IS SCALAR-ONLY AT EVAL, AND THE LOAD CHECK TRUSTS IT ANYWAY — two independent defects that meet on the first STRUCTURAL Ordered carrier. Found driving WI-858, which made anthill.prelude.Pair that carrier (before it, every Ordered provider was a host primitive or a witness over one, so nothing exercised this). DEFECT A — a builtin backs the load check without being able to handle the carrier. eval/builtins.rs registers PartialOrd.gt/gte/lt/lte and Ordered.compare on the SPEC ops, all routing through value_compare, which handles host SCALARS only ('expected Ordered scalars of matching type'). kb::typing::op_backed accepts kb.is_builtin(c) as backing with no check that the builtin applies to THIS carrier's values, so a concrete structural carrier providing PartialOrd passes check_provider_operations on the strength of an implementation that dies on its values. MEASURED on Pair: Ordered.compare(pair(2,1), pair(1,9)) answers 1 — because Pair.compare is a MEMBER and a carrier's own member overrides the builtin (WI-350/444) — while gt(pair(2,1), pair(1,9)), which ordered.anthill DERIVES from compare by rule and which Pair had no member for, died 'expected Ordered scalars of matching type, got Entity and Entity'. So the load was clean and the surface was half-broken. DEFECT B — the builtin SHADOWS the in-language derivation, so the derivation cannot be repaired in the language. ordered.anthill states the surface as laws (gt(a,b) <=> gt(compare(a,b),0), max(a,b) <=> ite(gte(a,b),a,b)) but a rule is not BACKING (WI-818), and MEASURED: giving Ordered.max/min real DEFAULT BODIES satisfies the load check and still fails at eval, because the body's own gte call hits the same scalar-only builtin. Removing just the Ordered.max/min registrations does not help either — the failure moves to PartialOrd.gte. INCONSISTENCY WORTH NOTING, since it is what makes the shape confusing: max/min are NOT resolver builtins (kb.is_builtin is false) so the load check DEMANDS members for them, while gt/lt/gte/lte ARE, so it demands nothing and they break silently — two halves of one surface treated oppositely. CURRENT WORKAROUND, shipped: pair.anthill spells all six members (compare + max/min + gt/gte/lt/lte), each a one-liner over compare. FIX DIRECTIONS, not yet chosen: make the ordered builtins FALL BACK to ordinary dispatch when the operands are not host scalars (most likely right — it preserves the scalar fast path); or register them per scalar carrier rather than on the spec op; or make op_backed verify a builtin's applicability to the carrier. ACCEPTANCE: a structural Ordered carrier gets a working compare/gt/gte/lt/lte/max/min with NO per-carrier members; pair.anthill's six members are DELETED and its Ordered surface still passes wi858_pair_orderings_test's full-surface arm (that deletion IS the acceptance); a load-time claim that an op is backed implies it runs for that carrier; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: rustland/anthill-core/src/eval/builtins.rs (value_compare and its callers), kb/typing.rs op_backed + check_provider_operations, stdlib/anthill/prelude/ordered.anthill's derivation block, WI-818 (a rule is not backing)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-30T18:41:14Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-876", author: "claude", content: "FIX SPACE NARROWED TO ONE OPTION, by driving each candidate. (1) GIVE THE SPEC DEFAULT BODIES — refuted. A builtin registered on a spec op beats that spec's own default body ALWAYS, not just for host scalars: MEASURED, with Ordered.max carrying a real body, the call on a Pair still died in value_compare, because the body's own gte call hit the PartialOrd.gte builtin. So the in-language derivation cannot be reached while the builtins are registered. (2) DROP THE BUILTIN REGISTRATIONS and rely on default bodies — refuted for a different reason: PartialOrd.gt/gte/lt/lte on Float are IEEE (a NaN operand answers false) and Float does NOT provide Ordered, so there is no compare for an in-language derivation to bottom out in. Removing the registrations would break the partial carrier the surface exists to serve. (3) MAKE THE BUILTINS FALL BACK TO DISPATCH when the operands are not host scalars — the remaining option, and the one with a working precedent IN THIS FILE: PartialEq.eq is already builtin-backed AND carrier-dispatching. builtin_eq calls semantic_equal, whose step 4 finds a head-carrier override (sem_eq_dispatch_target) and proves <carrier>.eq via prove_rule_predicate, with a separate branch through bridge_eq_op_to_eval for an override that has a RUNNABLE BODY, plus bridge-mode Suspend handling so an applicable-but-undecidable override never masquerades as a structural answer. THAT is exactly why eq works on a Pair today and compare does not: ordered_compare goes straight to value_compare with no dispatch step. WHAT THE WORK IS: a semantic_compare mirroring semantic_equal — same head-carrier lookup, same runnable-body vs rule-backed split, same bridge-mode discipline — with ordered_compare/gt/gte/lt/lte routed through it and the scalar path kept as the fast path. NOT a small edit, and it lands in a hot shared path (gt is called everywhere), which is why WI-858 shipped the per-carrier members instead. NOTE the asymmetry this explains: the reason eq needed no member on Pair while compare/gt/max all did is not a property of Pair — it is that ONE of the two builtin families learned to dispatch and the other did not.", at: "2026-07-30T16:59:53Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-876", author: "claude", content: "CORRECTION — the previous entry's fix space was wrong twice, and the right answer is the DICTIONARY. (a) 'Dropping the builtin registrations is refuted because Float needs IEEE gt' CONFLATED two different things: the registrations are on the SPEC OP (anthill.prelude.PartialOrd.gt), which is exactly what makes them shadow every carrier. Registering per SCALAR CARRIER instead — or bypassing them via the dictionary — keeps Float's IEEE gt and stops a structural carrier being intercepted. 'Drop the spec-op registration' is not 'have no builtin'. (b) 'Mirror semantic_equal' is the WRONG ALTITUDE. PartialEq.eq uses a value-directed head-carrier heuristic BECAUSE equality is coherent and fires from UNIFICATION — there is no call site to carry a dictionary (058 §3.7). Ordered is SELECTABLE: every dispatch has a call site and the typer already threaded the answer. Re-deriving the provider from the value's shape would recompute what the dictionary states, and would get it wrong wherever the value's head is not the selected provider (a witness ordering over a foreign carrier is exactly that case — ByLength over String). THE FIX: give builtins the frame's requirement dictionary. Frame.requirements (eval/frame.rs:180) already carries (__req_<spec>, RequirementHandle) pairs, and eval.rs:322 already reads one by name via find_requirement for every body that dispatches; the builtin ABI (fn(&mut Interpreter, &[Value])) is the only thing that does not see it. So: ordered_compare/gt/gte/lt/lte try the host-scalar path first (unchanged, still the fast path) and otherwise resolve their spec's handle from the current frame and invoke the provider's op through it. NOTE this generalizes past Ordered — any builtin registered on a SPEC op has the same hole, since a spec op is exactly the thing that can have per-carrier providers. ACCEPTANCE, restated: pair.anthill declares NO comparison operations at all (not gt/gte/lt/lte, not max/min, and not compare) and wi858_pair_orderings_test::the_whole_comparison_surface_works_on_a_pair still passes; Float's IEEE gt/lt/gte/lte are unchanged; the scalar fast path is preserved; full workspace green.", at: "2026-07-30T17:03:53Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-876", author: "claude", content: "FIX DIRECTION SUPERSEDED — the host implementation belongs in the LANGUAGE BINDING, keyed to the carrier, not registered on the spec op and not reached by teaching builtins to dispatch. The user's framing, and it is right: 'it should have defined compare in mapping'. WHY THE BUILTIN IS ON THE SPEC OP AT ALL — measured, and it is a grammar gap, not a choice: a 'provides X language rust' block admits artifact_clause, carrier_clause, namespace_map_clause, fact/rule declarations and proofs (tree-sitter-anthill/grammar.js, _provides_content) and NOTHING that maps an OPERATION to a host function. So rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/int64.anthill can assert 'fact Ordered[T = Int64]' and 'carrier { Int64: \"i64\" }' but has no way to say which host function is Int64's compare — and the registration had to go somewhere, so it went on anthill.prelude.Ordered.compare. That single registration is then the implementation for EVERY carrier that never wrote its own, which is exactly why it intercepts Pair. NOTE the check is not at fault: check_provider_operations deliberately SKIPS a host carrier (an anthill.realization.Implementation fact targeting X), on the correct grounds that the host artifact backs its ops. Int64 providing Ordered with no anthill-level compare is therefore legitimate — the implementation exists, it is in Rust. The defect is only WHERE it is keyed. WORK: add an operation-mapping clause to the binding block (the carrier_clause/namespace_map_clause pattern — one more _provides_content alternative), map compare/gt/gte/lt/lte/max/min for Int64/Float/String/BigInt there, and DELETE the spec-op registrations. Then give Ordered/PartialOrd the default BODIES that ordered.anthill today states only as laws — with the spec-op builtin gone they are no longer shadowed, and they terminate on the per-carrier builtin (gt(a,b) = gt(compare(a,b), 0), whose inner gt is on Int64). Float keeps its IEEE gt/lt/gte/lte as a per-carrier mapping, which is also more honest than a spec-op builtin that silently means IEEE for one carrier and total order for the rest. RESULT: a structural carrier needs ONLY compare — no max/min, no comparisons — and Pair gains 'provides PartialOrd/Ordered' plus that one operation. SCOPE NOTE: this is a language-surface increment (new binding clause) plus a stdlib migration, larger than the dictionary hack it replaces but correctly placed; the dictionary idea is recorded in the previous entry as the alternative that needs no grammar.", at: "2026-07-30T17:10:05Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-876", author: "claude", content: "CORRECTION TO THE PREVIOUS ENTRY — calling this a GRAMMAR gap was too narrow. The realization layer already has the vocabulary; what it lacks is an operation-to-host mapping, and the primitives' declared realization is EMPTY. MEASURED: (1) stdlib/anthill/realization/realization.anthill declares Implementation, CarrierBinding, NamespaceMapping, TypeMapping, LanguageMapping, EffectMapping, ReceiverRule — a full realization vocabulary — and OperationImpl, which despite the name marks an ANTHILL operation that has an expression body, NOT a host mapping. There is no entity mapping an operation to a host function. (2) Every primitive binding DECLARES an artifact: rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/{int64,bool,float,string,bigint}.anthill each name 'artifact \"rustland/anthill-stl/src/prelude/<x>.rs\"'. NONE OF THOSE FIVE FILES EXIST — rustland/anthill-stl/src/prelude/ contains only logical_stream.rs, meta.rs, mod.rs, stream.rs. (3) The loader reads the artifact path only to store it in the Implementation fact (kb/load.rs:15436-15515); nothing loads code from it. So the realization for the primitives is declared and empty. The REAL implementation is the hardcoded register_if_present list in eval/builtins.rs, keyed by SPEC-OP qualified name ('anthill.prelude.Ordered.compare'), which is how it ended up with no carrier key and therefore intercepting every carrier. THE WORK IS THEREFORE LARGER AND BETTER-DEFINED than 'add a clause': give the realization layer an operation-to-host mapping entity (the TypeMapping/NamespaceMapping pattern, keyed by lang + carrier + operation), a binding-block clause that produces it (the carrier_clause/namespace_map_clause pattern in _provides_content), and make the builtin registry read THOSE FACTS instead of a hardcoded spec-op list. That also removes the standing oddity that a binding's artifact path names a file that does not exist. NOTE the scale check before starting: the same hardcoded-by-spec-op-name registration serves every primitive operation (Numeric.add, String.concat, Bool.not, …), not just the ordering family, so migrating all of it at once is a large change — the ordering family is the one with a demonstrated defect and can go first, with the mechanism proved on it.", at: "2026-07-30T17:17:01Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-876", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. The fix is the one the last correction named — an operation-to-host mapping in the binding block — and it landed as: grammar `operation_map { compare: \"ordered_compare\" }` in `_provides_content`; a flat `anthill.realization.OperationMapping(carrier, operation, host_fn, lang)` fact emitted per entry; `register_operation_mappings` (eval/builtins.rs) reading those facts instead of the hardcoded spec-op names; the four spec-op ordering registrations DELETED; `Int64`/`String`/`BigInt` declaring and mapping `compare`+`gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte`, `Float` declaring and mapping the four IEEE ones and NO `compare`; and `PartialOrd.gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte` + `Ordered.max`/`min` gaining real DEFAULT BODIES. `host_fn` is a key into a CLOSED Rust registry, not a reflected symbol, and both failure modes are loud (unknown key; `<carrier>.<operation>` undeclared). PRE-FIX MEASURED FIRST, both halves exactly as the ticket said: the load refused the structural carrier for `max`/`min`, and with those hand-written `gt(pt(2,1), pt(1,9))` died 'expected Ordered scalars of matching type, got Entity and Entity'. THREE THINGS THE TICKET DID NOT PREDICT. (1) 'EXECUTABLE' HAD TWO DEFINITIONS AND ONE OF THEM WAS A BODY. `carrier_override_op` (eval's WI-444 override resolution) filtered on `op_has_runnable_body`, so a member whose implementation is the HOST's read as ABSENT and the SPEC DEFAULT ran instead of the carrier's own host code. Not hypothetical — it broke 7 tests: `gt(nan, 1.5)` fell into `PartialOrd`'s compare-based default and died `OperationBodyMissing {Ordered.compare}` (`Float` has no `compare`), and a `String` comparison inside a witness ordering fell through into `AmbiguousSpecOpDispatch` between `String.compare` and the program's two witnesses — SIX `wi844_sorted_set_driver_test` arms, which looked like a coexistence regression and was not. Both now share ONE predicate with the load check: `op_is_executable` = body | builtin | host mapping. Verified by INVERTING it — re-narrowing the filter re-breaks exactly two of this ticket's tests. (2) THE DERIVATION'S `requires` HAD EXACTLY ONE PLACE IT COULD LIVE, and I found it by hitting the other two. `PartialOrd.gt`'s default body reads `Ordered.compare`, which `PartialOrd` does not require — `MissingRequiresForSpecOp` at load. It cannot move to `Ordered`: `sort_ops` has ONE slot per (carrier, short name) and `build_sort_ops_table` pass 2 iterates a HashMap, so a carrier providing both specs would take whichever came first — a coin flip, not a rule. And `PartialOrd` cannot `requires Ordered[T]` at SORT level: `Float` provides one and not the other. What is left is the OP-SCOPED `requires Ordered[T]` (`list.anthill`'s `member` precedent), which says the right thing — this DERIVATION needs a total order — and demands nothing of a carrier supplying its own comparisons. (3) DECLARING CARRIER OPS MOVED SLD NAME RESOLUTION. A bare `gt(?a, 0)` in a rule inside `sort Int64` now resolves to `Int64.gt`, not the spec op, so it would have lost the resolver builtin it always had; `register_standard_builtins` gained carrier-keyed tags for `{Int64,BigInt,String,Float}.{gt,lt,gte,lte}`. Still a hardcoded list — that registry runs BEFORE `load_all` and has no facts to read — but no longer spec-op-keyed. `String.padRight`'s `gt(d, 0)` (on an `Int64`) had to be qualified for the same reason. ONE DELIBERATE BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: `Ordered.max(1.5, 2.75)` no longer resolves — `Float` provides `PartialOrd`, not `Ordered` (WI-644), and the old answer came from a spec-op builtin ignoring provisions AND using `total_cmp`, under which NaN ranks LARGEST. Correct refusal, poor diagnostic (an `Internal` from the unfilled requirement slot) — `eval_test::m3_float_comparison_and_max` pins `is_err()` and NOT the message, so the defect is not pinned: WI-883. PROCESS NOTE, a footgun: a `git stash` used to run a pre-change CONTROL silently left the generated tree-sitter parser stale (build.rs regenerated it from the reverted `grammar.js`, and `src/` is gitignored so the pop did not restore it). The suite still passed on the stale parser for unrelated reasons; caught by grepping `src/grammar.json` for the new rule. Regenerate after any stash that touches `grammar.js`. TESTS: `wi876_operation_mapping_test` (9) — the one-operation acceptance, the mapping facts, per-carrier `is_host_mapped_op`, the scalar controls on all three total carriers, Float IEEE incl. four NaN arms, the own-member-beats-default arm beside a rival ordering, and both loud failure modes. Full workspace 3821 passed / 0 failed via rustland/scripts/test.sh. FOLLOW-UPS FILED: WI-883 (use-site provision check / the diagnostic), WI-879 (fact-drive the SLD registry; `builtin_cmp`'s silent failure on non-numeric operands), WI-880 (migrate `Numeric`/`PartialEq`/the rest). WI-877 is UNBLOCKED and now costs one operation.", at: "2026-07-30T18:10:51Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-876", author: "user", content: "CODE-REVIEW PASS (4 agents: reuse / simplification / efficiency / altitude). FOUR THINGS I HAD WRONG, all found by review rather than by the suite, which was green throughout. (1) A SILENT SKIP IN THE CODE THAT EXISTS TO REMOVE SILENT SKIPS. `operation_map { squish: no_such_host_function }` written UNQUOTED — which `bindings` accepts, and which `carrier { T: i64 }` in the grammar's own doc comment TEACHES — produced a fact whose `host_fn` was not a string literal, the shape reader dropped it on a `continue`, and the mapping VANISHED: loaded clean, ran clean, registered nothing, no layer ever mentioning it. REPRODUCED myself before fixing. Now refused at the PRODUCER (`emit_operation_mapping_facts`), which makes the reader's skip unreachable by construction — the WI-805/808/809 'guard at each producer' discipline. (2) THE WRONG OWNER FOR THE OTHER REFUSAL. A mapping naming an operation the carrier does not DECLARE was caught only by the runtime's registration pass, so `anthill load` and `anthill check` — the tools whose job it is — both passed it silently. Split by who can answer: 'this operation does not exist' is now a LOAD error from `build_host_op_mappings`; 'this RUNTIME has no such host_fn' stays with the runtime, which is the only thing that can answer it and which must not judge a cpp-only mapping. (3) A FALSE CLAIM I WROTE TWICE. I said the resolver registry was 'no longer SPEC-OP-KEYED'. It is not: WI-876 ADDED the carrier entries BESIDE the spec-op ones and deleted nothing (a bare `gt(?x, 5)` in another namespace still resolves to the spec op). MEASURED by the reviewer: `PartialOrd.gt(\"b\", \"a\")` as a rule-body goal yields NO SOLUTIONS while eval answers `true` — the ticket's own defect, alive in the SLD engine. Corrected in `kb/mod.rs` and CLAUDE.md and pushed into WI-879 with the measurement. Same for kernel-language.md's 'a mapping makes the operation executable for the backing check' — true by construction, UNREACHED in fact, because `check_provider_operations` skips a host carrier wholesale (WI-880). (4) MAX/MIN LEFT INCONSISTENT WITH MY OWN RATIONALE. I host-mapped `gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte` for the scalar fast path and then let `max`/`min` fall to the spec default — an interpreter frame plus a dictionary dispatch where the host answers in one call, four of six keyed to the host and two not. Now mapped on all three total carriers. ALSO APPLIED: the mappings are CACHED on the KB (`register_standard_builtins` re-runs for every fresh interpreter, and `run_in_bridge_interp` builds one per bridged evaluation — a fact walk there cost a named-args clone and four linear scans per mapping, per crossing); `op_is_executable` asks its two O(1) host legs before `op_has_runnable_body`, which clones a whole `OpInfoRecord` to read one `is_some()`; `is_host_mapped_op` fast-paths the empty set and the raw symbol before hashing a qualified name; `read_string_field` (duplicated in `proof_verify.rs`) became the shared `get_named_string_arg`; the two fact emitters now derive the block's carrier QN and language through ONE helper instead of maintaining agreement by comment; a doc comment I had orphaned (my items were spliced INTO `build_sort_ops_table`'s doc block) was moved back; the stale `float_pair` pointer in `resolve.rs` and the spec-op names in the moved builtins' `expect_args` labels were corrected. HOT PATH, the acceptance's 'scalar fast path preserved', now traced rather than assumed: `gt(1, 2)` is statically PinNow'd to `Int64.gt` (`PartialOrd.gt` having a body makes `lookup_spec_op_dispatch` return None, opening the WI-444 block) and hits the builtin in ONE step; the dictionary route reaches `Int64.compare` in one step likewise. Timed control on the heaviest binary: 74.6s/1890 tests at baseline vs 80.2s/1899 after — the +9 are this ticket's own tests, each a full stdlib load (~0.5s), so the residual is inside noise. FINAL: full workspace 3823 passed / 0 failed; scaland 238/239, its one failure PRE-EXISTING (`relation.anthill`, untouched here, last modified by WI-818) — scaland needed only an operation-COUNT update for Float (28 -> 32). PROCESS: two `git stash` round-trips for pre-change controls each left the generated tree-sitter parser stale (build.rs regenerates from the reverted `grammar.js`; `src/` is gitignored so the pop does not restore it). Regenerate and VERIFY by grepping `src/grammar.json` for the new rule after any stash touching `grammar.js`.", at: "2026-07-30T18:41:01Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-876", author: "user", content: "CODE-REVIEW FINDINGS WORKED (15 raised; each VERIFIED by probe before fixing, since a review is a claim like any other). FOUR PRODUCER HOLES, all reproduced first and all now refused. (a) THE TARGET WAS ONLY CHECKED TO RESOLVE, NEVER TO BE AN OPERATION — an entity constructor is a qualified `<ns>.<Sort>.<entity>` name, so `operation_map { pt: \"ordered_compare\" }` over `entity pt(x, y)` resolved, registered, and would have made the CONSTRUCTOR run a comparison, every builtin lookup consulting the builtin map FIRST. A `const` (`Float.nan`) resolves the same way. Now gated on `kind_of == SymbolKind::Operation`, LOAD error. (b) NO ARITY AGREEMENT between the declared operation and the host function — and MY OWN CONTROL TEST enshrined it, mapping a 1-argument `squish` to the 2-argument `ordered_compare` and asserting the program LOADS CLEAN; it then died `ArityMismatch` on the call, i.e. this ticket's own defect class one level down. `host_fn_by_key` now returns a `HostFn { arity, f }` and registration refuses a mismatch, naming both arities. The check is at REGISTRATION, not load, because that is the earliest point that knows both: the loader knows the operation's arity but not another language's function set. (c) `operation_map` UNDER `language anthill` was silently dropped — loads clean, runs clean, registers nothing, which is verbatim the shape this ticket removes. Now refused, naming the block. (d) A REPEATED KEY silently last-won: both facts were emitted and the runtime's HashMap insert let the second overwrite the first, so `gt` could quietly acquire IEEE semantics. Refused as SYNTAX via the existing `check_label_unique`, per WI-805/808/809's discipline — and applied to `carrier` and `namespace_map` too, which had the identical hole. ONE PREDICATE SPLIT IN TWO. `op_is_executable`'s `kb.is_builtin` leg reads the RESOLVER's BuiltinTag map, and this ticket made the two registries maximally divergent — `PartialOrd.gt` is in the resolver's and NOT the interpreter's, its eval registration having moved per carrier. So an eval-side gate trusting it would select a carrier member the interpreter cannot call and skip the spec default that would have worked. Now `op_is_interpretable` (body | host mapping) for `carrier_override_op`, `op_is_executable` (+ resolver builtin) for `op_backed`. Unreachable today — every carrier-keyed resolver tag is also mapped — but WI-880 is exactly where that coincidence ends. ALSO: the host-mapping index canonicalized while the interpreter's builtin map is a RAW lookup, so the predicate could promise a spelling the map lacked; both now key BOTH spellings, the eq-index precedent. A SECOND SILENT SKIP FOUND AND CLOSED: a MALFORMED hand-asserted `OperationMapping` fact hit a bare `continue` — the producer guard cannot see a hand-written fact, so my 'unreachable by construction' comment was false. Now a load error. NOTE the wider consequence, documented but NOT closed: a WELL-FORMED hand-asserted fact is indistinguishable from a loader-emitted one, so a program can rebind a carrier's operation without a binding block. That is a property of the whole realization vocabulary (`Implementation` is equally hand-assertable) and wants a provenance rule. TWO CLAIMS CORRECTED rather than fixed, because the code was right and the comment was not: `value_compare`'s `total_cmp` Float arm STAYS — `ordered_*` is the TOTAL family and a float-valued TOTAL carrier (the `TotalFloat` shape) needs exactly that, transitivity being unavailable from `partial_cmp`; the carrier now picks its family in a visible `operation_map` line instead of a branch buried in shared code. And the unknown-`host_fn` refusal keeps its full blast radius (it stops every interpreter, including the resolver's per-bridge one) because a binding naming a function the runtime lacks is broken for the whole program — the MESSAGE now says so, since it will often surface at a call unrelated to the mapping. SCALAND: the `operation_map` clause was missing from the Scala parser, so scaland could not read any shipped binding file. Added (grammar + IR + loader arm) with its own parse test. TEST COST: the suite did ~25 full stdlib loads where 4 do — the fresh-interpreter-per-call rule guards a TRAPPED call, and these all succeed. wi876 went 4.31s -> 1.21s. NOT DONE, and flagged: `float.anthill` still carries `import Ordered.{max}` and `rule abs(?a) <=> max(?a, neg(?a))`, both now DEAD for `Float` since `Ordered.max` is unreachable there — restating the law over `PartialOrd` is a stdlib semantics decision, not a cleanup, and is left to the user (the `int64.anthill` half, a shadowed import, was unambiguous and is gone). And the two new load errors carry no SPAN: `pb.span` is in hand, but 25 sibling `LoadError::Other` uses in the same file are equally unspanned, so a purpose-built spanned variant is a file-wide cleanup rather than this ticket's — the messages name the carrier and the block instead. FINAL: rustland 3827 passed / 0 failed; scaland 239/240, the one failure PRE-EXISTING (`relation.anthill`'s `fix[R](p:` form, untouched here).", at: "2026-07-30T19:47:12Z")
